{"answer":"The critic's case concedes the key structural points: it acknowledges that the target defines a monotone Horn-fragment least Herbrand model and that the attacker rule 'discharges an attack when the attacker is refuted.' The objection then rests on the claim that reinstatement as a derived property requires a 'native mechanism' showing that discharge *causes* a previously underivable node to become derivable, and that the target does not exhibit the derivation rule combination yielding it. But this demand is not established as necessary. In a least Herbrand model of Horn rules, the model is constructed as the least set closed under the rules; if a node's derivability depends on an attacker being refuted, and that refutation is itself derivable, then the node is in the least model. The 'monotone, never retracts' observation does not undermine this: it is precisely because derivability is additive that once the refutation of the attacker is in the model, any node whose only obstruction was that attacker becomes derivable. The critic has not shown that this requires a deletion or negation-as-failure operation; it requires only that the rules encode the dependency, which the attacker rule does by construction. The critic also does not establish that the target 'merely labels' the rule without any rule combination — it identifies two rules whose interaction in the least Herbrand model yields the effect. The claim that the guarantee is 'unmet' is therefore not established; it rests on an unsupported requirement that reinstatement be exhibited as a specific syntactic rule combination rather than as a semantic consequence of the least model construction. No fact is conceded beyond what the target itself states."}